1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,514
Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:10,514 --> 00:00:22,106
Tonight we are going back to London, on a November evening around the year eighteen eighty, when a thick, soft, yellow fog has settled over the whole city.

3
00:00:22,106 --> 00:00:24,798
Nothing dramatic is going to happen.

4
00:00:24,798 --> 00:00:33,922
You will simply walk home through the fog after a long day, stopping now and then, until you reach a warm room and a fire.

5
00:00:35,722 --> 00:00:40,592
Before we set out, let your body settle into wherever you are lying.

6
00:00:40,592 --> 00:00:43,743
Let your shoulders drop away from your ears.

7
00:00:43,743 --> 00:00:53,770
Take one slow breath in, as if you were breathing the cool, damp evening air, and then breathe out for a little longer than you breathed in.

8
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:55,059
Once more, slowly.

9
00:00:55,059 --> 00:00:55,417
Good.

10
00:00:55,417 --> 00:00:58,784
That is all the effort tonight will ask of you.

11
00:01:00,584 --> 00:01:05,636
If you lose the thread of the story, that is perfectly all right.

12
00:01:05,636 --> 00:01:09,990
The fog is thick, and it is easy to lose your way in it.

13
00:01:09,990 --> 00:01:22,194
Just let the voice carry on beside you like a quiet companion, and if you fall asleep somewhere along the walk, you will still arrive home safely in the end.

14
00:01:31,994 --> 00:01:40,613
It is a little after five o'clock, and you are standing on the top step of an office near the Strand, buttoning your coat.

15
00:01:40,613 --> 00:01:45,912
Behind you, the clerks are covering their ledgers and turning down the gas.

16
00:01:45,912 --> 00:01:49,515
In front of you, the street has almost disappeared.

17
00:01:49,515 --> 00:01:59,194
The fog came creeping up from the river in the middle of the afternoon, and now it fills the whole road like milky tea poured into a cup.

18
00:02:00,994 --> 00:02:04,538
Londoners have many names for a fog like this.

19
00:02:04,538 --> 00:02:12,089
Some call it a pea-souper, because it is thick and yellowish green, like a bowl of split pea soup.

20
00:02:12,089 --> 00:02:14,785
Others call it a London particular.

21
00:02:14,785 --> 00:02:30,349
Charles Dickens used that phrase in his novel Bleak House, when a young clerk explains to a visitor from the country that the fog outside is simply a London particular, as if it were a local speciality.

22
00:02:32,149 --> 00:02:34,830
The fog is not only made of water.

23
00:02:34,830 --> 00:02:42,480
London in eighteen eighty is a city of several million people, and nearly every house burns coal.

24
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:48,632
On a cold, still evening, the smoke from all those chimneys has nowhere to go.

25
00:02:48,632 --> 00:02:59,989
It sinks and mingles with the river mist, and turns the air the colour of weak mustard, faintly sooty, with a smell of coal fires and wet stone.

26
00:03:01,789 --> 00:03:10,074
On days like this, the gas in the shop windows is sometimes lit at noon, and stays lit until closing time.

27
00:03:10,074 --> 00:03:27,581
The shopkeepers along the Strand have been working all afternoon by its light, and their windows glow softly as you pass, full of gloves and umbrellas and books and pastries, each one a little lit stage floating in the dusk.

28
00:03:29,381 --> 00:03:34,431
You step down into it, and the first thing you notice is the quiet.

29
00:03:34,431 --> 00:03:35,863
Fog swallows sound.

30
00:03:35,863 --> 00:03:49,957
The wheels of the carts and the hooves of the horses, which usually make the Strand a roaring place, are softened now to a muffled clopping, as if the whole city had put on felt slippers.

31
00:03:49,957 --> 00:03:56,816
Voices come out of the fog close by, and then fade away again before you can see the faces.

32
00:03:58,616 --> 00:04:06,640
You turn down a narrow lane towards the river, because the walk along the water is the gentlest way home.

33
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:10,767
The walls on either side are damp and black with soot.

34
00:04:10,767 --> 00:04:17,569
A cat sits on a window sill, tucked into itself, watching you pass with half-closed eyes.

35
00:04:17,569 --> 00:04:25,517
Ahead, at the bottom of the lane, there is a soft glow, where the lamps along the river are already lit.

36
00:04:27,317 --> 00:04:32,707
Then you hear it, faint and far away, coming through the fog from the west.

37
00:04:32,707 --> 00:04:37,234
It is the great clock at Westminster, chiming the quarter hour.

38
00:04:37,234 --> 00:04:45,283
Its four slow notes drift over the water, softened and blurred, as if someone were playing them under a blanket.

39
00:04:45,283 --> 00:04:53,834
Many people in this city set their evening by those chimes, and tonight you will hear them more than once on your walk.

40
00:04:55,634 --> 00:05:11,704
The clock tower is only about twenty years old in eighteen eighty, and the great bell inside it, which everyone already calls Big Ben, has a crack in it, so its voice is a little deeper and rougher than it was meant to be.

41
00:05:11,704 --> 00:05:19,594
At night, the four faces of the clock are lit from behind, so that they glow like pale moons above the river.

42
00:05:19,594 --> 00:05:23,069
Tonight, in the fog, you cannot see them at all.

43
00:05:24,869 --> 00:05:29,318
At the bottom of the lane you come out onto the river wall.

44
00:05:29,318 --> 00:05:47,115
The fog here is a little thinner, moving in slow banks over the water, and the long line of lamps along the embankment fades away into it, each one a little fainter than the one before, until the last ones are only soft smudges of gold.

45
00:05:47,115 --> 00:05:50,810
You stop for a moment, and simply stand and look.

46
00:06:00,610 --> 00:06:10,679
Turning away from the river for a little while, you walk up a side street of tall brick houses, and you meet the lamplighter on his round.

47
00:06:10,679 --> 00:06:25,272
He is a thin man in a cap and a long coat, with a ladder on his shoulder for the lamps that need cleaning, and in his hand a long pole with a little flame burning at its tip, like a candle on a stick.

48
00:06:27,072 --> 00:06:31,691
London has been lit by gas for more than sixty years by now.

49
00:06:31,691 --> 00:06:48,936
Under the pavements runs a network of iron pipes, carrying gas from the great gasworks by the river, where the coal is baked in hot ovens and the gas is stored in huge round holders that rise and sink as they fill and empty.

50
00:06:48,936 --> 00:06:55,018
From there it travels quietly under the streets, to tens of thousands of lamps.

51
00:06:56,818 --> 00:07:05,343
The lamplighter stops beneath a lamp post, reaches up with his pole, and turns the little tap inside the glass.

52
00:07:05,343 --> 00:07:15,481
Then he touches the flame to the burner, and with a soft pop the lamp comes alive, a small fan of yellow light inside the glass box.

53
00:07:15,481 --> 00:07:21,087
In clear weather it would throw a neat circle of light onto the pavement.

54
00:07:21,087 --> 00:07:24,850
Tonight the fog turns it into a great round halo.

55
00:07:26,650 --> 00:07:30,160
He walks on to the next lamp without hurrying.

56
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:40,536
He has done this round every evening for years, in rain, in snow and in fog, and his feet know the way even when his eyes cannot see it.

57
00:07:40,536 --> 00:07:45,190
He knows every lamp on the round as if it were an old friend.

58
00:07:45,190 --> 00:07:51,141
At dawn he will walk the same streets again, turning them all out, one by one.

59
00:07:52,941 --> 00:07:56,831
His work does not end with lighting and putting out.

60
00:07:56,831 --> 00:08:07,605
In the daytime he climbs his ladder to polish the glass panes of each lamp, trims the burners, and replaces any glass broken by a careless cart.

61
00:08:07,605 --> 00:08:19,053
A clean lamp gives a better light, he will tell you, and in weather like this, every little bit of light is a kindness to someone finding their way home.

62
00:08:20,853 --> 00:08:29,820
Behind him, the street is slowly filled with light, lamp after lamp, like a string of pearls being laid down in the fog.

63
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:45,962
Children used to follow lamplighters on their rounds, just to watch them work, and a small boy is doing it now, trotting a few steps behind with his hands in his pockets, until his mother calls him in from a doorway.

64
00:08:47,762 --> 00:08:56,605
The houses along this street are tall and narrow, built of brick that was once yellow and is now dark grey with soot.

65
00:08:56,605 --> 00:09:14,365
Each one has the same front door with a half-moon of glass above it, called a fanlight, and the same flight of steps, and the same iron railings in front, guarding a narrow space that drops down to the kitchen windows below the street.

66
00:09:16,165 --> 00:09:31,387
In one of those kitchens, below the railings, you can see a cook moving about in the lamplight, lifting the lid of a pan, and the warm smell of onions and gravy drifts up through the fog to the pavement.

67
00:09:31,387 --> 00:09:41,359
Somewhere above, a curtain is drawn across a window, and the shadow of someone reaching up to close it moves softly behind the blind.

68
00:09:43,159 --> 00:09:48,946
At the corner stands a red pillar box, its paint shining wet in the lamplight.

69
00:09:48,946 --> 00:10:02,523
Letters put in it tonight will be collected late in the evening by a postman with a lantern, carried through the fog to the sorting office, and delivered across London by the morning.

70
00:10:02,523 --> 00:10:11,277
In this city, there are several posts a day, and a letter written after breakfast can often be answered before supper.

71
00:10:21,077 --> 00:10:26,853
You make your way back down to the river, and walk west along the embankment.

72
00:10:26,853 --> 00:10:32,703
This broad, quiet road beside the water is still quite new in eighteen eighty.

73
00:10:32,703 --> 00:10:41,404
Only ten years ago, the river here ran up against mud banks and old wharves, and at low tide the smell was terrible.

74
00:10:41,404 --> 00:10:49,130
Then the engineers built this great granite wall out into the water, and filled in the space behind it.

75
00:10:50,930 --> 00:10:56,599
The man behind it was Joseph Bazalgette, the engineer of London's new sewers.

76
00:10:56,599 --> 00:11:10,439
Hidden under your feet, inside the embankment, there is a great brick tunnel carrying the city's waste away downstream, and beside it, a railway tunnel, where steam trains run underground.

77
00:11:10,439 --> 00:11:20,157
Above it all is this wide road, with young plane trees along it, and a promenade beside the river, where people can walk in the air.

78
00:11:21,957 --> 00:11:34,446
All along the top of the river wall, at regular intervals, there are cast iron lamp posts, and at the foot of each one two plump iron fish curl their tails around the column.

79
00:11:34,446 --> 00:11:39,901
People call them the dolphin lamps, though they look a little like sturgeon.

80
00:11:39,901 --> 00:11:49,519
They were designed for the embankment by an architect named George Vulliamy, and tonight each one wears a crown of misty golden light.

81
00:11:51,319 --> 00:11:55,966
Not everyone along this stretch of river trusts gas any longer.

82
00:11:55,966 --> 00:12:08,431
For the last couple of years, part of the embankment has been lit as an experiment with a new kind of lamp, an electric one, which burns with a hard, bluish white glare.

83
00:12:08,431 --> 00:12:12,192
People come down in the evening just to look at it.

84
00:12:12,192 --> 00:12:18,093
But it is costly, and most of London still prefers the gentle yellow of the gas.

85
00:12:19,893 --> 00:12:32,364
Below the lamps, set into the face of the granite wall just above the water, there are great bronze lion heads, each one holding a heavy mooring ring in its mouth.

86
00:12:32,364 --> 00:12:34,276
Boats can tie up to them.

87
00:12:34,276 --> 00:12:43,840
The story goes, among the watermen, that if the river ever rises high enough for the lions to drink, London will be in flood.

88
00:12:43,840 --> 00:12:47,818
Tonight the tide is low, and the lions are very dry.

89
00:12:49,618 --> 00:12:59,194
A little further along, standing on its own by the river wall, there is something very old and very strange to find in London.

90
00:12:59,194 --> 00:13:06,034
It is a tall stone needle, an obelisk, carved in Egypt more than three thousand years ago.

91
00:13:06,034 --> 00:13:11,582
People call it Cleopatra's Needle, though it is far older than Cleopatra.

92
00:13:11,582 --> 00:13:17,586
It was raised here only two years ago, in the autumn of eighteen seventy eight.

93
00:13:19,386 --> 00:13:29,760
When it was set up, a time capsule was buried in its base, filled with things that might one day tell people what life in London was like.

94
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:41,411
There are newspapers and coins, a railway guide, a box of hairpins, a baby's feeding bottle, a set of children's toys, a razor, and photographs of the day.

95
00:13:41,411 --> 00:13:48,101
You think about them lying there in the dark, under the old pink stone, as you walk past.

96
00:13:49,901 --> 00:13:59,036
Out on the river, the fog lies low over the water, and through it you can hear the quiet sounds of the Thames at night.

97
00:13:59,036 --> 00:14:01,800
Water slaps gently against the wall.

98
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:07,327
Somewhere a chain rattles softly as a barge settles against its mooring.

99
00:14:07,327 --> 00:14:19,533
Then the low, puffing breath of a little steam tug comes out of the fog, and you see its lit wheelhouse slide slowly past, like a lantern carried on the water.

100
00:14:21,333 --> 00:14:32,375
Ahead of you, across the river to the left, there is the dim shape of a long bridge, with the lamps along it making a faint curve of gold in the air.

101
00:14:32,375 --> 00:14:40,971
A train is crossing it, very slowly, and you can hear the soft beat of its engine and see the glow from its firebox.

102
00:14:40,971 --> 00:14:46,677
Then it is gone into the fog on the other side, and the river is quiet again.

103
00:14:56,477 --> 00:15:07,183
Where the embankment meets a wider street, there is a cab rank, a line of hansom cabs waiting along the kerb, their lamps glowing in the fog.

104
00:15:07,183 --> 00:15:18,648
A hansom is a light, two-wheeled carriage, with room for two passengers inside, and the driver sitting high up behind, holding the reins over the roof.

105
00:15:18,648 --> 00:15:24,722
The statesman Benjamin Disraeli is said to have called it the gondola of London.

106
00:15:26,522 --> 00:15:34,458
The horses stand patiently in the shafts, with thick woollen rugs over their backs to keep off the damp.

107
00:15:34,458 --> 00:15:43,538
One of them has a nosebag on, and is quietly eating its oats, its ears turning now and then towards a sound in the fog.

108
00:15:43,538 --> 00:15:53,381
Every so often it breathes out through its nostrils, and two little clouds of steam rise into the lamplight and slowly disappear.

109
00:15:55,181 --> 00:16:03,905
Beside the rank stands a small wooden hut, painted dark green, with a pitched roof and windows all along its sides.

110
00:16:03,905 --> 00:16:11,492
Warm light shines through the glass, and a thin stove pipe on the roof breathes out a wisp of smoke.

111
00:16:11,492 --> 00:16:22,189
This is a cabmen's shelter, one of the first of its kind in London, put up so that cab drivers waiting for a fare have somewhere warm to sit.

112
00:16:23,989 --> 00:16:31,234
The first shelters were built only a few years ago, by a charity set up in eighteen seventy five.

113
00:16:31,234 --> 00:16:40,048
Until then, a cab driver on a cold night had nowhere to go but a public house, and could not leave his horse for long.

114
00:16:40,048 --> 00:16:51,552
The shelters were built on the roadway itself, and so, by the rule of the police, each one could be no bigger than a horse and cab standing in the street.

115
00:16:53,352 --> 00:17:03,578
Inside, about a dozen men can sit around a narrow table, and a man in an apron looks after a little stove, a kettle and a frying pan.

116
00:17:03,578 --> 00:17:16,035
He sells mugs of hot tea and coffee, bread and butter, rashers of bacon and chops, all at a fair price, and the cab drivers can bring their own food to be cooked.

117
00:17:16,035 --> 00:17:17,650
There are rules, too.

118
00:17:17,650 --> 00:17:21,725
No drinking of spirits, no gambling, and no swearing.

119
00:17:23,525 --> 00:17:28,733
You cannot go inside, because the shelters are only for the cabmen.

120
00:17:28,733 --> 00:17:40,625
But the door opens for a moment as a driver comes out, wiping his mouth, and you catch a breath of warm air, smelling of tea, frying bacon and damp wool.

121
00:17:40,625 --> 00:17:50,341
Inside you glimpse men in thick coats, one reading a newspaper, one cutting bread, another asleep with his chin on his chest.

122
00:17:52,141 --> 00:17:58,927
In the roof of each hansom, just in front of the driver's seat, there is a small trapdoor.

123
00:17:58,927 --> 00:18:11,066
The passenger, sitting snugly inside behind folding wooden doors, can push it open to speak to the driver, and pay the fare through it at the end of the journey.

124
00:18:11,066 --> 00:18:18,530
On a foggy night, a passenger may sit there with the trap open, giving directions street by street.

125
00:18:20,330 --> 00:18:29,302
The driver climbs up into his high seat at the back of his cab, settles his rug over his knees, and takes up the reins.

126
00:18:29,302 --> 00:18:34,203
Somewhere along the rank a passenger has hailed him from the fog.

127
00:18:34,203 --> 00:18:45,738
He clicks his tongue softly, and the horse leans into its collar, and the cab rolls away, its two lamps swaying a little, until the fog closes around it.

128
00:18:47,538 --> 00:18:57,975
From somewhere in the west, the clock at Westminster chimes again through the fog, the full eight notes of the half hour, slow and blurred.

129
00:18:57,975 --> 00:19:12,541
A cab driver standing by the shelter door takes out his watch, looks at it, and puts it back in his pocket with a satisfied nod, as though the clock had only just confirmed what he already knew.

130
00:19:22,341 --> 00:19:29,170
At the corner, near the shelter, there is a chestnut seller with a little iron brazier on legs.

131
00:19:29,170 --> 00:19:40,529
The coals glow orange through the holes in its sides, and on top sits a flat, perforated pan full of chestnuts, their shells split and blackened at the edges.

132
00:19:40,529 --> 00:19:49,946
The man turns them slowly with his hands, which seem not to feel the heat at all, and every now and then one of them cracks softly.

133
00:19:51,746 --> 00:19:53,989
You stop and buy a pennyworth.

134
00:19:53,989 --> 00:20:01,390
He scoops them up and twists them into a little cone of paper, and they are almost too hot to hold.

135
00:20:01,390 --> 00:20:03,932
You peel one with your fingertips.

136
00:20:03,932 --> 00:20:10,062
Inside it is pale gold and soft and floury, and it tastes faintly sweet and smoky.

137
00:20:10,062 --> 00:20:18,136
You stand beside the brazier for a while, with the warmth of the coals on your face, eating them one by one.

138
00:20:19,936 --> 00:20:26,062
A little way along, there is another stall that you can find by its smell alone.

139
00:20:26,062 --> 00:20:37,549
It is the baked potato man, with a tall, shining tin can on legs, heated inside by a small charcoal fire, with a little chimney poking out of the top.

140
00:20:37,549 --> 00:20:41,225
In the oven at its heart, potatoes are kept hot.

141
00:20:41,225 --> 00:20:48,117
He splits one open for a customer, adds butter and a pinch of salt, and wraps it in paper.

142
00:20:49,917 --> 00:21:04,502
Writers who described London's streets in these years said that people often bought hot potatoes not only to eat, but to warm their hands on the way home, and then ate them when they had gone cold.

143
00:21:04,502 --> 00:21:07,833
On a night like this, you can understand why.

144
00:21:07,833 --> 00:21:16,200
A young clerk walks off down the street holding one in each hand inside his coat pockets, like two little stoves.

145
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:21,264
And then, through the fog, you hear a bell.

146
00:21:21,264 --> 00:21:27,945
It is not the clock this time, but a small handbell, rung slowly as someone walks along.

147
00:21:27,945 --> 00:21:37,132
Out of the fog comes the muffin man, carrying a wide wooden tray on his head, covered with a cloth to keep in the warmth.

148
00:21:37,132 --> 00:21:43,813
Under the cloth are rows of soft round muffins, ready to be toasted by the fire at home.

149
00:21:45,613 --> 00:21:53,103
A door opens on the other side of the street, and a maid comes hurrying down the steps with a plate.

150
00:21:53,103 --> 00:22:02,841
The muffin man stops, lifts the tray down from his head, folds back the cloth, and counts out half a dozen muffins onto her plate.

151
00:22:02,841 --> 00:22:12,279
They exchange a few words about the fog, and she says she can hardly see her own hand in it, and then she hurries back inside.

152
00:22:14,080 --> 00:22:17,384
You decide to buy two for yourself, for later.

153
00:22:17,384 --> 00:22:26,003
The muffin man wraps them for you in a piece of paper, warm and soft, and you tuck them carefully into your coat pocket.

154
00:22:26,003 --> 00:22:40,512
Then he settles the tray back on his head, picks up his bell, and walks slowly away down the street, its gentle ringing growing fainter and fainter until it is just a little shimmer of sound in the fog.

155
00:22:42,312 --> 00:22:53,762
Down near the river, a coffee stall is being set up for the night, a painted wooden cart with a big copper urn on top and a lamp hanging from its roof.

156
00:22:53,762 --> 00:23:00,208
Stalls like this stay open until the early morning, for the late workers of the city.

157
00:23:00,208 --> 00:23:10,066
The stall keeper sets out thick white mugs and slices of bread and butter, and a cab driver is already waiting, rubbing his hands.

158
00:23:11,866 --> 00:23:20,385
Further along the street, a policeman is walking his beat, in a long cape glistening with damp, and a tall helmet.

159
00:23:20,385 --> 00:23:30,397
At his belt hangs a lantern with a round glass lens in front, called a bull's eye, which can throw a beam of light where he points it.

160
00:23:30,397 --> 00:23:40,112
He walks at the same slow, steady pace he always walks, trying the handles of shop doors as he passes, to be sure they are locked.

161
00:23:49,912 --> 00:23:57,515
Now you turn away from the busy streets, and walk up into the quieter part of the town, where you live.

162
00:23:57,515 --> 00:24:02,904
The roads grow narrower, and the sounds of the city fall away behind you.

163
00:24:02,904 --> 00:24:14,125
Your own footsteps on the wet pavement are the loudest thing you can hear, and even they seem to be absorbed by the fog almost as soon as you make them.

164
00:24:15,925 --> 00:24:24,700
Here and there, at a crossing, a boy is standing with a lighted torch, made of a stick wrapped in rags dipped in pitch.

165
00:24:24,700 --> 00:24:35,615
On the very thickest nights of fog, boys like this come out to guide people through the streets for a penny, walking ahead with the flame held high.

166
00:24:35,615 --> 00:24:43,210
These are called link boys, and their trade is very old, from the days before streets had lamps at all.

167
00:24:45,010 --> 00:24:50,990
At the edge of a muddy crossing stands an old man with a broom, a crossing sweeper.

168
00:24:50,990 --> 00:25:07,922
All day long he sweeps a clean path across the road, through the mud and the straw and the leavings of the horses, so that ladies and gentlemen can cross without spoiling their shoes and hems, and they give him a coin if they are kind.

169
00:25:07,922 --> 00:25:12,893
Tonight he leans on his broom in the fog, nodding to you as you pass.

170
00:25:14,693 --> 00:25:19,228
You come out at last into a small square, which is your square.

171
00:25:19,228 --> 00:25:30,027
In the middle there is a garden behind iron railings, where a few tall plane trees stand with their branches bare, and fallen leaves lie in the grass.

172
00:25:30,027 --> 00:25:36,363
The garden is locked at night, and only the people who live around the square have keys.

173
00:25:36,363 --> 00:25:41,402
It is very still in there, a little island of darkness inside the fog.

174
00:25:43,202 --> 00:25:52,679
The plane trees of London have been planted in squares and along streets all through the century, partly because they are so tough.

175
00:25:52,679 --> 00:26:05,918
Their bark peels away in patches, so that the soot of the city does not choke them, and underneath there is fresh, pale bark, cream and green and grey, like the pattern on an old map.

176
00:26:05,918 --> 00:26:09,101
In the fog, their trunks look almost silver.

177
00:26:10,901 --> 00:26:17,528
The houses around the square are quieter and a little grander than the ones by the river.

178
00:26:17,528 --> 00:26:21,623
Most of the windows are lit, behind curtains or blinds.

179
00:26:21,623 --> 00:26:35,398
From one of them, on the first floor, comes the sound of someone playing the piano, softly, a slow tune you almost recognise, the notes coming out muffled through the glass and the fog.

180
00:26:35,398 --> 00:26:37,781
You walk slowly past, listening.

181
00:26:39,581 --> 00:26:46,835
On the corner of the square, a gas lamp stands over the steps of the house where you have your rooms.

182
00:26:46,835 --> 00:26:57,106
Your landlady keeps a respectable house, and on foggy evenings she leaves a lamp burning in the hall, so that her lodgers can find the keyhole.

183
00:26:57,106 --> 00:27:03,858
You climb the four steps, feeling for each one with your foot, and fit your key into the lock.

184
00:27:05,658 --> 00:27:14,853
As you open the door, the clock at Westminster chimes one more time, far away across the rooftops, a faint and wavering sound.

185
00:27:14,853 --> 00:27:25,727
It is the hour now, and after the long chime of sixteen notes, the great bell strikes slowly, each deep note rolling out over the fog and dying away.

186
00:27:25,727 --> 00:27:32,368
You count them in your head, as you always do, and then you step inside and close the door.

187
00:27:34,168 --> 00:27:40,242
Inside, the hall smells of beeswax polish and coal smoke and a little of supper.

188
00:27:40,242 --> 00:27:42,292
The gas is turned down low.

189
00:27:42,292 --> 00:27:49,961
A coat stand holds the other lodgers' coats, still damp, and an umbrella stand holds their umbrellas.

190
00:27:49,961 --> 00:27:55,580
From the kitchen below comes the comfortable clink of dishes being washed.

191
00:27:55,580 --> 00:28:01,730
You take off your wet coat and hang it up, and climb the stairs to your own room.

192
00:28:11,530 --> 00:28:17,642
Your room is on the first floor, at the front of the house, overlooking the square.

193
00:28:17,642 --> 00:28:32,296
The landlady's maid has already lit the fire for you, and it is burning well in the black iron grate, the coals glowing red and orange between the bars, with little blue flames dancing along the top.

194
00:28:32,296 --> 00:28:37,450
A brass fender stands in front of it, shining softly in the firelight.

195
00:28:39,250 --> 00:28:41,346
You turn up the gas a little.

196
00:28:41,346 --> 00:28:56,736
On the wall beside the fireplace there is a bracket with a glass shade, shaped like a tulip, and as you turn the little tap, the flame inside it grows brighter, filling the room with a warm, slightly yellow light.

197
00:28:56,736 --> 00:29:04,829
Then you turn it down again, low, because the firelight is enough, and the room feels more peaceful in its glow.

198
00:29:06,629 --> 00:29:10,666
The room is crowded in the comfortable way of the time.

199
00:29:10,666 --> 00:29:24,098
There is a round table with a fringed cloth, an armchair by the fire with a crocheted cover on its back, called an antimacassar, to keep the hair oil of its sitter off the upholstery.

200
00:29:24,098 --> 00:29:36,944
There is a bookshelf, a little writing desk, and on a stand by the window, an aspidistra, the tough green houseplant that grows happily in every dim, gaslit parlour in London.

201
00:29:38,744 --> 00:29:52,055
On the mantelpiece, under a glass dome, there is a small clock, and beside it a pair of china dogs, a few letters tucked behind a candlestick, and a picture postcard of the sea.

202
00:29:52,055 --> 00:30:05,517
On the wall, a round clock with a long pendulum ticks slowly, the brass disc of its pendulum swinging back and forth behind a little window, catching the firelight on every swing.

203
00:30:07,317 --> 00:30:17,603
On the little table beside the armchair there is a book waiting for you, the second volume of a long novel, borrowed from a circulating library.

204
00:30:17,603 --> 00:30:31,532
For a small yearly fee, these libraries lend out new books, usually in three volumes, one at a time, so that a reader may wait several days, and wonder, before the next part of the story arrives.

205
00:30:31,532 --> 00:30:35,818
You open it to your bookmark, and then put it aside for now.

206
00:30:37,618 --> 00:30:39,910
Now it is time for the muffins.

207
00:30:39,910 --> 00:30:51,740
You take the long toasting fork from its hook beside the grate, split one muffin carefully with your fingers, and hold the halves out towards the glowing coals.

208
00:30:51,740 --> 00:30:56,916
Slowly the surface turns golden, and then a little brown at the edges.

209
00:30:56,916 --> 00:31:00,391
The smell of warm toasted bread fills the room.

210
00:31:00,391 --> 00:31:05,714
You butter them while they are hot, and the butter melts into the holes.

211
00:31:07,514 --> 00:31:18,093
The kettle has been sitting on the hob at the side of the grate, and now it begins to sing softly, a thin column of steam curling from its spout.

212
00:31:18,093 --> 00:31:26,776
You make yourself a cup of cocoa, stirring the dark powder into the hot water with a little sugar and a splash of milk.

213
00:31:26,776 --> 00:31:36,698
Cocoa has become a popular evening drink in these years, warm and gentle, and sold as a wholesome, nourishing thing to drink before bed.

214
00:31:38,498 --> 00:31:48,491
You sit down in the armchair, with your cocoa in one hand and a plate of muffins on your knee, and stretch your feet out towards the fender.

215
00:31:48,491 --> 00:31:55,058
Your boots are still a little damp, and soon you can see a faint steam rising from them too.

216
00:31:55,058 --> 00:32:04,909
The landlady's cat, a large tabby with white paws, has crept in through the door behind you, and now settles on the hearthrug with a sigh.

217
00:32:06,709 --> 00:32:13,618
Outside, beyond the window and the lace curtains, the fog presses softly against the glass.

218
00:32:13,618 --> 00:32:21,666
The street lamp in the square makes a big, blurry halo of gold in it, and nothing else can be seen at all.

219
00:32:21,666 --> 00:32:30,246
It feels as if this room were the only room in the world, warm and lit and small, floating in a great soft cloud.

220
00:32:30,246 --> 00:32:31,460
The fire shifts.

221
00:32:31,460 --> 00:32:32,675
The clock ticks.

222
00:32:32,675 --> 00:32:33,738
The cat purrs.

223
00:32:43,538 --> 00:32:57,694
When the cocoa is finished, you take the candlestick from the mantelpiece, light the candle from the fire with a twist of paper, and turn the gas right down until it goes out with a tiny sigh.

224
00:32:57,694 --> 00:33:09,565
In the next room your bed is waiting, and the maid has already put a stone hot water bottle between the sheets, so that the bed is warm right down to the bottom.

225
00:33:11,365 --> 00:33:18,887
You undress by the candlelight and climb into bed, and pull the heavy blankets up around your shoulders.

226
00:33:18,887 --> 00:33:24,890
The sheets are cool at the edges and warm in the middle, where the bottle has been.

227
00:33:24,890 --> 00:33:32,340
Through the open door, you can still see the red glow of the parlour fire, settling slowly into embers.

228
00:33:32,340 --> 00:33:38,416
Let your body sink down into that warmth now, and let your arms and legs grow heavy.

229
00:33:40,216 --> 00:33:47,357
Far away across the city, the lamplighter has finished his round and gone home to his own fire.

230
00:33:47,357 --> 00:33:50,589
The cab horses are dozing under their rugs.

231
00:33:50,589 --> 00:33:53,896
The chestnut seller has covered his brazier.

232
00:33:53,896 --> 00:34:01,037
The muffin man's bell is silent, and the policeman walks his slow beat along the quiet streets.

233
00:34:01,037 --> 00:34:08,930
All over London, behind millions of lit windows, people are settling down for the night, just as you are.

234
00:34:10,730 --> 00:34:17,974
Let your breath slow down, until it is as soft and as even as the ticking of the parlour clock.

235
00:34:17,974 --> 00:34:18,812
In and out.

236
00:34:18,812 --> 00:34:19,651
In and out.

237
00:34:19,651 --> 00:34:30,707
If a thought comes to you, let it drift away into the fog like the sound of the muffin man's bell, growing fainter and fainter, until it is gone.

238
00:34:30,707 --> 00:34:33,452
There is nothing more to do tonight.

239
00:34:33,452 --> 00:34:34,901
Everything is done.

240
00:34:36,701 --> 00:34:39,606
And while you sleep, the city sleeps too.

241
00:34:39,606 --> 00:34:51,013
Some time before morning, perhaps, a little wind will come up the river from the sea, and the fog will begin to thin, and lift, and drift away over the rooftops.

242
00:34:51,013 --> 00:35:01,429
By the time the lamplighter comes round again to put out the lamps, the sky may be pale and clear, and the first carts will rattle into the square.

243
00:35:01,429 --> 00:35:04,050
But that is a long, long way off yet.

244
00:35:05,850 --> 00:35:11,669
Thank you for walking home through the fog with The Quiet Archive tonight.

245
00:35:11,669 --> 00:35:20,711
The candle is out, the fire is low, and the great clock at Westminster will keep watch over the city until morning.

246
00:35:20,711 --> 00:35:21,576
Sleep well.

247
00:35:21,576 --> 00:35:22,362
Goodnight.
